ss backend) will include modules for IAM/Security (JWT/OAuth2, RBAC), academic management (Teachers, Students), and enrollment (critical quota logic).\n\n\n**Detailing System Components**\n\nI\u0027m now detailing each module\u0027s internal mechanisms, moving past structure. I\u0027m focusing on concurrency controls (optimistic/pessimistic locking) and data integrity within the database. I\u0027m deep diving into the specific functionality of the modules. I\u0027m outlining how the grading, reporting/search, and audit modules will function, and their respective requirements. I\u0027m also planning the integration of OpenAPI/Swagger documentation. I am evaluating the RDBMS database (PostgreSQL) and in-memory cache (Redis) layers, ensuring support for ACID properties and appropriate indexing for efficient querying.\n\n\n**Refining Technical Specifications**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the technical specifications, particularly the concurrency control strategies for enrollment and grading. I\u0027ve detailed transaction approaches using pessimistic locking with `SELECT...FOR UPDATE` or optimistic locking with atomic updates. For grading, I\u0027m ensuring RBAC middleware to enforce teacher authorization and filtering student queries to show only published grades. I am also planning the logging module using database triggers and application interceptors to capture a comprehensive audit trail with relevant context.\n\n\n**Evaluating Database Solutions**\n\nI am now evaluating the database solutions. I\u0027m choosing PostgreSQL over NoSQL due to the need for strict ACID properties and referential integrity, critical for managing enrollments and grades. Furthermore, I\u0027ve opted for a stateless authentication method using JWT within an HttpOnly Secure Cookie and RBAC to enable horizontal scalability. I\u0027m also planning to utilize Redis for caching to accelerate read operations, especially during enrollment and grade query periods. Finally, I\u0027ve also decided to use containers for deployment to ensure portability across different environments.\n\n\n\n",
